Module 5 – Frontend – HTML5 

Theory Assignment
 • Question 1: Difference b/w HTML & HTML5?
 •             HTML5 is far more dynamic and incorporates multimedia components than previous iterations of HTML, which mainly allowed you to construct static websites that needed to be spiced up using CSS and JavaScript. Video and audio are naturally supported, and it can even be used to create animations or games.

 • Question 2: What are the additional tags used in HTML5?
 •             
1. <article>
Represents a self-contained piece of content, such as a blog post, news article, or forum post.
2. <aside>
Represents content that is tangentially related to the content around it, like sidebars, pull quotes, or related links.
3. <details>
Used to specify a control that the user can open and close to reveal additional information.
4. <summary>
Used in conjunction with <details> to provide a heading that the user can click to expand or collapse the content.
5. <figcaption>
Provides a caption or description for the content within a <figure> element.
6. <figure>
Represents content like images, illustrations, diagrams, videos, or code snippets, along with an optional caption (<figcaption>).
7. <footer>
Represents the footer of a document or section, typically containing metadata, copyright information, or contact details.
8. <header>
Represents introductory content or a set of navigational links. It can be used for a section of the document or a whole page.
9. <main>
Represents the dominant content of the <body>, excluding headers, footers, sidebars, and navigation links.
10. <mark>
Used to highlight text that is of particular relevance to the user (often for search results).
11. <meter>
Represents a scalar measurement within a known range (e.g., disk usage, a progress bar, or a rating).
12. <nav>
Represents a section of the page intended for navigation links (menus, links to other sections of the site).
13. <progress>
Represents the completion progress of a task, such as file upload or download, or any other process.
14. <section>
Represents a thematic grouping of content, often with its own heading. Used for structuring content into sections.
15. <summary>
Provides a summary for the <details> tag, which can be clicked to show or hide more content.
16. <time>
Represents a specific time or range of time. Can be used for dates, times, durations, etc.
17. <video>
Represents a video player, allowing for easy embedding of video content. Supports multiple formats and can include controls like play, pause, etc.
18. <audio>
Represents audio content. Similar to <video>, it allows embedding audio files with controls for playback.
19. <source>
Specifies multiple media resources for <video> and <audio> elements, allowing the browser to choose the appropriate file format.
20. <track>
Used with <video> and <audio> elements to specify text tracks, such as subtitles, captions, or descriptions.
21. <wbr>
Suggests an optional line break, allowing the browser to insert a break at a suggested location when the line is too long.
22. <template>
Defines a template that is not rendered when the page loads, but can be cloned and used at runtime by JavaScript.
23. <datalist>
Provides an autocomplete feature on <input> elements, allowing a predefined set of options to be shown when typing.
24. <output>
Represents the result of a calculation or user action, often used in forms.
25. <svg>
Used to define scalable vector graphics (SVGs), allowing for dynamic, resolution-independent graphics on the web.
26. <canvas>
Used to draw graphics on the fly via JavaScript, such as for games, charts, or other interactive graphics.
27. <bdi>
Represents text that may be formatted in a different direction than its surrounding text (Bi-Directional Isolation).
28. <bdo>
Represents a span of text whose directionality (left-to-right or right-to-left) can be explicitly controlled.
29. <progress>
Used to display the progress of a task (e.g., file upload).
30. <output>
Represents the result of a calculation or user action, often used within a form.